  "surprise": "P34 ALiBi (per-head linear distance bias, slopes 1/2..1/256, replacing RoPE in all six Peri-LN blocks; 4,800,000 params identical to 107) scored 619/613/585 on seeds 0/1/2 vs 107-claude's 582/571/608: exact means 605.67 vs 606.00 rounded, +18.7 over 587.00, paired +37/+42/-23, inside the registered -45..+45 range (central +8). Keep under the strict three-seed rule; with a 3-seed-mean sd of ~25 this is not established on fresh seeds. Pretraining as predicted: 4276/4169/4194 steps (+30% vs 107's 3264/3208/3277; below 111 NoPE's 4716 because the float SDPA mask replaces the flash kernel and costs one bias build plus six bf16 casts per forward), final val loss 0.0804/0.0829/0.0813 vs 0.0822/0.0827/0.0836 (minima 0.0791/0.0760/0.0751, all rising into the endpoint), held-out greedy 0.980/0.985/0.983 vs 0.9815/0.977/0.9785. The registered mechanism is REFUTED in its direction: the RL start was the worst of any Lean-protocol run, not better. Round-1 targets solved 381/661/224 vs 107's 722/689/679 and NoPE's 556/568/464; round-1 sample acc 0.118/0.248/0.060 vs 0.29; round-1 parse failures 23768/14324/30896 of 48,000 (50/30/64%) vs 107's 12155/7575/17990 (26%), so at T=0.8 the recency-biased model initially emits malformed 7+-line chains far more often than RoPE or NoPE. Surprise: expert iteration then climbed about twice as fast as under any previous incumbent. Targets solved r2 904/908/530, r3 1054/1037/899, r4 1104/1097/1033 (107: r4 1089/1031/1083), an r1->r4 gain of +723/+436/+809 (mean +656) vs 107's +371 and NoPE's +403; parse failures fell to 5600/5910/21478 by r2 and 3540/4140/4647 by r4 (107 averaged ~15% across passes), RL-solved mean 1078 vs 1068, and the final eval reached more long proofs (ge10 87/99/77, ge11 18/23/16, ge12 4/4/3 vs 107 s0's 82/16/3; L* 11 on every seed; eval parse failures 11735/11616/12228 of 70,912 vs 107's ~13,400 mean). So ALiBi helped by making the model more plastic under the fixed RL AdamW (or by giving expert iteration cleaner length-7+ signal once syntax was fixed), not by starting closer to the target distribution; seed 2's -23 came from a round-1 start of 224 that RL could not fully recover in four rounds, which caps how much luck is in the +18.7. Speed: RL sampling 275-386 samples/s vs 194-249 and RL rounds 178-239 s vs 266-322 s, totals 1485/1548/1433 s vs 1845/1799/1946 (RL is fixed in samples, so the speed bought nothing there). Follow-ups: (a) the slow start suggests the strongest heads (slopes 1/2, 1/4) over-localize at unseen lengths; a gentler slope set (2^-2..2^-9, or ALiBi on half the heads with the rest position-free) could keep the RL climb and fix the round-1 collapse; (b) P34 x P31-style: the bias is a mask, so learned per-head slopes (8 params, AdamW) are a one-line P36-style variant; (c) 3 fresh seeds (3,4,5) would test whether +18.7 survives.",
